1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,099
Lesson 6.6.

2
00:00:02,479 --> 00:00:04,814
Landscape: inference and beyond.

3
00:00:05,917 --> 00:00:07,921
We close the unit with a landscape.

4
00:00:08,301 --> 00:00:11,006
The same device of coupled oscillators is

5
00:00:11,006 --> 00:00:13,712
useful for much more than linear systems.

6
00:00:14,092 --> 00:00:18,746
We will see three examples: sampling
Bayesian posterior distributions,

7
00:00:18,746 --> 00:00:23,600
solving constrained quadratic programs
and computing matrix exponentials.

8
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:26,575
And we will finish with an
idea that tries to explain

9
00:00:26,575 --> 00:00:28,778
where the thermodynamic
advantage comes from.

10
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:36,039
With a Gaussian prior and linear
observations with Gaussian noise, the

11
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:41,402
posterior is also Gaussian, with
precision Λ = I + Hᵀ H / σ².

12
00:00:41,782 --> 00:00:45,382
If we load Λ as the coupling
matrix, the equilibrium of

13
00:00:45,382 --> 00:00:49,047
the device is exactly the
posterior: nothing needs to be

14
00:00:49,047 --> 00:00:52,188
solved, every instant of
the system is a sample.

15
00:00:52,568 --> 00:00:56,235
Aifer and colleagues propose
circuits for this model and for

16
00:00:56,235 --> 00:00:59,719
Bayesian logistic regression,
and argue that the sampling

17
00:00:59,719 --> 00:01:02,836
time scales only as the
logarithm of the dimension.

18
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:07,030
We simulate it in two dimensions
with three observations.

19
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:13,547
The device samples reproduce the
exact posterior: the variances

20
00:01:13,547 --> 00:01:19,099
are 0.27 and 0.64, and the
estimated ones, 0.28 and 0.67.

21
00:01:19,479 --> 00:01:21,631
The uncertainty is not
computed separately:

22
00:01:21,631 --> 00:01:23,633
it comes for free from the fluctuations.

23
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:29,760
If there are also box constraints, such
as every variable between zero and one, it

24
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,097
is enough to add walls: when the
system hits one, it stays at the edge.

25
00:01:34,477 --> 00:01:36,835
At low temperature, the
average converges to

26
00:01:36,835 --> 00:01:38,817
the optimum of the quadratic program.

27
00:01:39,197 --> 00:01:44,136
In our example, the optimum is 0.6 and 1;

28
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:48,955
with k_B T = 0.001 we get 0.60 and 0.99.

29
00:01:49,335 --> 00:01:52,839
At higher temperature the wall
biases the average inward.

30
00:01:53,950 --> 00:01:57,377
The third example uses the
dynamics, not only the equilibrium.

31
00:01:57,757 --> 00:02:04,309
In an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process, the
autocorrelation at lag s is e^(−A s) Σ.

32
00:02:04,689 --> 00:02:09,182
By measuring correlations between
the current state and the one s units

33
00:02:09,182 --> 00:02:13,358
earlier, and multiplying by β A,
we obtain the matrix exponential.

34
00:02:13,738 --> 00:02:16,660
With dimension five and
a trajectory of twenty

35
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:19,899
thousand units, the relative
error is five percent.

36
00:02:20,279 --> 00:02:24,002
Duffield and colleagues show that
this gives a speedup linear in

37
00:02:24,002 --> 00:02:27,143
the dimension for simulating
linear dynamical systems.

38
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:31,761
Why does a gain proportional
to the dimension always appear?

39
00:02:32,141 --> 00:02:37,133
Duffield and colleagues propose a
hypothesis: thermodynamic parallelism.

40
00:02:37,513 --> 00:02:42,336
Noise pushes the system in all directions
at once, so a single trajectory

41
00:02:42,336 --> 00:02:47,358
explores all modes simultaneously and
estimates many quantities in parallel.

42
00:02:47,738 --> 00:02:50,200
Noise, which in a digital computer is an

43
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:53,032
enemy, is here the
resource that parallelizes.

44
00:02:53,412 --> 00:02:56,293
It is a hypothesis, not
a theorem, and it is

45
00:02:56,293 --> 00:02:58,846
among the open problems of unit eleven.

46
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:05,708
These primitives touch artificial
intelligence in Bayesian deep learning, in

47
00:03:05,708 --> 00:03:10,785
constrained optimization, in
state-space models, and in unit eight,

48
00:03:11,165 --> 00:03:15,063
where the natural gradient will
use the same linear-algebra core.

49
00:03:16,183 --> 00:03:20,867
Common mistakes: sampling the posterior
at a temperature other than one.

50
00:03:21,247 --> 00:03:24,147
Solving a quadratic program
at high temperature.

51
00:03:24,527 --> 00:03:27,054
Estimating the exponential with very long

52
00:03:27,054 --> 00:03:29,519
lags, where the signal is lost in noise.

53
00:03:29,899 --> 00:03:33,100
And treating thermodynamic
parallelism as a theorem.

54
00:03:34,217 --> 00:03:38,886
In summary: the equilibrium of an
oscillator device is an exact Bayesian

55
00:03:38,886 --> 00:03:43,556
posterior in the Gaussian case; with
walls and low temperature it solves

56
00:03:43,556 --> 00:03:48,096
quadratic programs; and its
autocorrelation gives matrix exponentials.

57
00:03:48,476 --> 00:03:52,383
Thermodynamic parallelism is the
hypothesis that unifies these advantages.

58
00:03:53,483 --> 00:03:56,554
To practice: derive
the Gaussian posterior.

59
00:03:56,934 --> 00:03:59,399
Explain why the temperature must be one.

60
00:03:59,779 --> 00:04:02,151
Prove the autocorrelation formula.

61
00:04:02,531 --> 00:04:04,825
And explain what biases
the solution of the

62
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:06,959
quadratic program at finite temperature.
